but why this is not good na bro
I completely get why that feels frustrating—especially when PC players are ready to jump in on day one. Rockstar has followed this console-first pattern for years, and there are a few main business and technical reasons behind it:
Why Rockstar Delays the PC Version
Hardware Optimization. Consoles have fixed specs, making it much easier for developers to polish a massive, demanding game without crashes. PCs have thousands of different graphics cards and processors, which takes extra time to test and optimize.
Maximizing Sales ("Double-Dipping"). Releasing on consoles first encourages impatient fans to buy the game on PS5 or Xbox now, and then buy it a second time later for better graphics and mods on PC.
Protecting the Launch Experience. Keeping the game on closed console systems at launch helps prevent early data mining, leaks, and multiplayer cheating before the game is established.
Storefront Cuts. Launching on their own Rockstar Games Launcher first—or holding off on Steam—helps Rockstar keep more of the revenue instead of immediately paying a 30% cut to third-party stores.
The Silver Lining for PC Players
A More Polished Game. By the time the PC version arrives, major launch bugs and server issues from the console release are usually fixed.
Better Visuals & Mods. The PC version almost always launches with unlocked frame rates, higher graphical settings, and eventual modding support that consoles do not get.
Are you thinking about picking up a console just to play it at launch, or are you planning to hold out for the PC release? Let me know if you want to compare the cost of a budget console setup versus waiting!




